3-This page describes what a Flue agent does out of the box: the tools the model
4-gets, what environment it runs in, how incoming messages are handled, what its
5-context window contains, and the limits the runtime enforces. It is a map of
6-runtime behavior, not an authoring API — the pages that define these behaviors
7-are linked from each section. Flue's inner agent loop builds on
8-[pi's](https://pi.dev) agent core; everything below is Flue's own contract, and
9-where you want the pi coding agent's equivalents, see
10-[pi's usage docs](https://pi.dev/docs/latest/usage).
11:## Built-in tools
12-An agent with a [sandbox](/docs/guide/sandboxes/) attached gets six tools that
13-operate on it. These are the tools the model calls — application code and
14-sandbox adapters use the [`Sandbox`](/docs/reference/sandbox-api/#sandbox)
15-surface these tools are built on, which is deliberately lower-level
16-(whole-file verbs, no truncation).
17-### `read`
18:Reads a file. Parameters: `path`, optional `offset` (line number to start
19-from, 1-indexed), optional `limit` (maximum lines).
20-- Output is truncated to **2000 lines or 50 KB**, whichever is hit first, and
21:  never mid-line. Truncated output ends with a marker naming the shown range
22-  and the offset to continue from (`Use offset=N to continue.`), so the model
23-  can page through files of any length.
24:- An `offset` past the end of the file is an error naming the file's actual
25-  line count.
26-- A single line larger than the byte budget is surfaced as its first 50 KB
27-  with a note that the remainder is not reachable via `offset`/`limit`.
28-### `write`
29:Writes a file whole. Parameters: `path`, `content`. Creates the file and any
30-missing parent directories; overwrites silently when the file exists.
31-### `edit`
32:Exact-text replacement. Parameters: `path`, `oldText`, `newText`, optional
33-`replaceAll`.
34-- `oldText` must match exactly one region of the file: zero matches is an
35:  error telling the model to check whitespace and indentation; multiple
36:  matches is an error asking for more surrounding context — unless
37-  `replaceAll` is set, which replaces every occurrence and reports the count.
38-- The read → replace → write transaction is atomic per file: tool calls in
39-  one batch run in parallel, and same-file mutations from `write` and `edit`
40:  are serialized through a per-path lock so a genuine conflict surfaces as a
41:  "could not find" error instead of a silently lost edit. A `bash` command
42-  mutating the same file concurrently is not synchronized.
43-### `bash`
44:Executes a shell command in the sandbox. Parameters: `command`, optional
45-`timeout` (seconds).
46-- Returns combined stdout/stderr, truncated to the **last** 2000 lines or
47:  50 KB (the tail is where errors and final results live). A non-zero exit
48-  appends the exit code.
49-- A command that exceeds `timeout` returns a recoverable exit-124 result
50-  rather than failing the operation, so the model can react.
51-### `grep`
52:Searches file contents. Parameters: `pattern` (regex), optional `path`,
53-`include` (glob filter), `literal`.
54-- Runs `rg` inside the sandbox when available (probed once), falling back to
55-  POSIX `grep -E`.
56:- Returns matching lines with file paths and line numbers, capped at **100
57-  matches** and **500 characters per line**; hitting the cap is reported with
58-  advice to narrow the search.
59-### `glob`
60:Finds files by name. Parameters: `pattern`, optional `path`. Uses shell
61:`find -name` semantics — the pattern matches file names, not paths — and
62:returns up to **1000** paths.
63-### Framework tools
64-Independent of any sandbox, the framework adds its own tools when the
65-capability exists: `task` for [subagent delegation](/docs/guide/subagents/)
66-(always present; inert until agents are declared), `activate_skill` when the
67-agent has [skills](/docs/guide/skills/), and `read_skill_resource` when an
68-imported skill packages resource files. These names are reserved — a custom
69-tool can't take them.
70-A sandbox adapter may replace the six sandbox tools with its own set — see
71-[Sandbox-provided tools](/docs/guide/sandboxes/#sandbox-provided-tools) —
72-so check an integration's documentation before assuming ordinary file or
73-command tools are present.
74-## Environment defaults
75-An agent has **no sandbox unless you attach one** with
76-[`useSandbox()`](/docs/reference/agent-hooks-api/#usesandbox), and at most
77:one. Without a sandbox: the six file and shell tools aren't in the tool set,
78-no workspace context enters the system prompt, workspace skills aren't
79-discovered, and [`harness.sandbox`](/docs/reference/agent-api/#harnesssandbox)
80-throws. Everything else — custom tools, imported skills, subagents, state —
81-works the same either way.
82-Attaching one defines several behaviors at once (tools, workspace discovery,
83-skills, what subagents inherit) — the [Sandboxes
84-guide](/docs/guide/sandboxes/#what-a-sandbox-adds) walks through them.
85-Presence is re-read at every turn boundary, so a conditional `useSandbox()`
86-can attach or detach the environment mid-conversation; the swap is narrated
87-to the model as an [`environment`
88-signal](/docs/reference/agent-api/#dynamic-resources).
89-## Message handling
90-Every input — HTTP prompt, `dispatch()`, channel delivery, scheduled trigger
91-— is admitted as a **submission**, recorded durably before any model work
92:begins. Submissions for one conversation form a queue processed in admission
93-order, and the agent does not sit idle behind a busy conversation's turn:
94-- One submission runs at a time.
95-- A message that arrives while the agent is busy **joins the live response at
96-  the next turn boundary** when it can, and otherwise waits its turn as its
97-  own submission. Nothing is dropped: a delivery that misses the live
98-  response runs on its own afterward.
99:- Every accepted submission reaches exactly one durable terminal outcome —
100-  `completed`, `failed`, or `aborted` — no matter how many crashes happen in
101-  between.
102-Retries, recovery, and abort mechanics are the [Durability
103-guide](/docs/guide/durability/)'s territory; the wire contract (the `202`
104-admission response, streaming) is in [Routing](/docs/guide/routing/).
105-## Context composition
106-At initialization the runtime composes the system prompt from what it finds:
107-the agent function's returned instructions, and — when a sandbox is attached
108:— the working directory path, a directory listing, the contents of
109-`AGENTS.md` when present, plus the discovered skill, subagent, and tool
110-rosters.
111-The system prompt is then **frozen**: it keeps describing the workspace and
112-catalogs discovered at initialization until the next compaction rebaselines
113-it against the current environment. Mid-window changes — tools mounting or
114-unmounting, skills flipping, the environment swapping — are narrated to the
115-model as append-only [signals](/docs/reference/agent-api/#dynamic-resources)
116-instead of prompt rewrites, which keeps the transcript's earlier turns
117-consistent with the prompt they actually ran under (and keeps the system
118:prompt's share of the provider's prompt cache warm, though a tool change
119-invalidates the cache through the native tools array).
120-## Context management
121-When the conversation approaches the model's context window, the runtime
122-compacts: older messages fold into a summary and recent ones are preserved
123-verbatim. Threshold compaction triggers when used tokens exceed the window
124-minus a model-aware reserve (capped at 20,000 tokens); the most recent 8,000
125-tokens are kept verbatim by default. Both knobs, the summarization model, and
126-opting out are [`CompactionConfig`](/docs/reference/agent-hooks-api/#compactionconfig);
127-overflow recovery and explicit
128-[`harness.compact()`](/docs/reference/agent-api/#harnesscompact) compact even
129-when threshold compaction is disabled.
130-## Limits
131-The numbers the runtime enforces, collected from the sections above plus the
132-delegation machinery:
133-| Limit                          | Value                                                                                                      |
134-| ------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
135:| `read` output                  | 2000 lines / 50 KB, head-truncated with continuation marker                                                |
136:| `bash` output                  | 2000 lines / 50 KB, tail-truncated                                                                         |
137-| `grep` results                 | 100 matches, 500 chars per line                                                                            |
138:| `glob` results                 | 1000 paths                                                                                                 |
139-| Delegation depth               | 4 — a `task` chain (including harness invocations) deeper than this fails with `delegation_depth_exceeded` |
140-| Compaction reserve             | model-aware, capped at 20,000 tokens                                                                       |
141-| Kept verbatim after compaction | 8,000 tokens by default                                                                                    |
142-Tool-set size has no framework cap, but every mounted tool spends context —
143-see [Conditional tools](/docs/guide/tools/#conditional-tools) for keeping the
144-set lean.

